						<div class="posenc-scale-bar"></div>
						<span class="posenc-scale-label">+1</span>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						Each row in the heatmap is a position's encoding — its unique fingerprint.
						Notice the striped pattern: low-numbered dimensions (left) oscillate rapidly
						across positions, while higher dimensions (right) change slowly. No two rows
						are identical, so the model can always tell positions apart.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* LINKAGE: embedding + position = input to attention */}
				<div class="posenc-handoff">
					<div class="posenc-handoff-label">The input to attention:</div>
					<div class="posenc-handoff-row">
						<div class="posenc-handoff-box posenc-handoff-embed">
							<span class="posenc-handoff-icon">E</span>
							<span>Token Embedding</span>
						</div>
						<span class="posenc-handoff-plus">+</span>
						<div class="posenc-handoff-box posenc-handoff-pos">
							<span class="posenc-handoff-icon">P</span>
							<span>Position Encoding</span>
						</div>
						<span class="posenc-handoff-eq">=</span>
						<div class="posenc-handoff-box posenc-handoff-result">
							<span class="posenc-handoff-icon">&rarr;</span>
							<span>Input Vector</span>
						</div>
					</div>
					<p class="posenc-handoff-note">
						Each token now carries both its <strong>meaning</strong> (from the embedding) and its
						<strong>position</strong> (from the positional encoding). These combined vectors are what
						flow into the attention mechanism — the subject of the next section.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			{/* ===== Section 04: Attention — Q, K, V ===== */}
			<section id="attention" class="mb-24 scroll-mt-20">
				<div class="mb-8 flex items-baseline gap-4">
					<span class="font-mono text-sm text-accent/60">04</span>
					<div>
						<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-accent-2 sm:text-3xl">Attention</h2>
						<p class="mt-1 text-base text-textColor/60">The mechanism that lets tokens talk to each other</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				{/* LINKAGE: Receiving combined vectors from Feature #7 */}
				<div class="linkage-callout linkage-attention">
					<span class="linkage-icon">&#8599;</span>
					<span>We're now zooming into the <strong>Multi-Head Attention</strong> block in the architecture diagram. The input vectors — carrying both meaning and position — arrive here.</span>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						Each input vector knows <em>what</em> a token means and <em>where</em> it sits — but
						so far, tokens are completely independent of each other. The word “bank” has the
						same representation whether it appears in “river bank” or “bank account.”
						Attention is how tokens <strong>talk to each other</strong>, gathering context from the
						rest of the sequence to resolve ambiguities like this.
					</p>
					<p>
						The mechanism works through three learned projections. Each input vector is multiplied
						by three different weight matrices — <strong class="qkv-q-text">W<sub>Q</sub></strong>,
						<strong class="qkv-k-text">W<sub>K</sub></strong>, and
						<strong class="qkv-v-text">W<sub>V</sub></strong> — to produce three new vectors:
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Q, K, V analogy cards */}
				<div class="qkv-analogy-cards">
					<div class="qkv-card qkv-card-q">
						<div class="qkv-card-letter">Q</div>
						<div class="qkv-card-name">Query</div>
						<div class="qkv-card-desc">“What am I looking for?”</div>
					</div>
					<div class="qkv-card qkv-card-k">
						<div class="qkv-card-letter">K</div>
						<div class="qkv-card-name">Key</div>
						<div class="qkv-card-desc">“What do I contain?”</div>
					</div>
					<div class="qkv-card qkv-card-v">
						<div class="qkv-card-letter">V</div>
						<div class="qkv-card-name">Value</div>
						<div class="qkv-card-desc">“What do I offer?”</div>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						Think of it like a library lookup. Each token broadcasts a <strong class="qkv-q-text">query</strong>
						(“I'm looking for adjectives that describe me”), every token has a
						<strong class="qkv-k-text">key</strong> (“I'm an adjective”), and every token holds
						a <strong class="qkv-v-text">value</strong> (“here's my information”). When a query
						matches a key, the corresponding value gets pulled in — that's how context flows
						between tokens.
					</p>
					<p>
						Below, watch how three input tokens get projected through the weight matrices.
						Press <strong>Step</strong> to advance one step at a time, or <strong>Play</strong>
						to see the full animation.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Interactive QKV projection visualization */}
				<div class="qkv-viz-container">
					<div class="qkv-controls">
						<button id="qkv-step-btn" class="qkv-btn qkv-btn-step">
							<svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="none"><path d="M3 2L11 7L3 12V2Z" fill="currentColor"/></svg>
							Step
						</button>
						<button id="qkv-play-btn" class="qkv-btn qkv-btn-play">
							<svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="none"><path d="M2 1L12 7L2 13V1Z" fill="currentColor"/><rect x="11" y="1" width="2" height="12" rx="0.5" fill="currentColor"/></svg>
							Play
						</button>
						<button id="qkv-reset-btn" class="qkv-btn qkv-btn-reset">
							<svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="none"><path d="M2 7A5 5 0 1 1 7 12" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" fill="none"/><path d="M2 10V7H5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" fill="none"/></svg>
							Reset
						</button>
						<span id="qkv-step-label" class="qkv-step-label">Step 0 / 9</span>
					</div>
					<div class="qkv-viz-scroll">
						<svg id="qkv-svg" class="qkv-svg" viewBox="0 0 810 420" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
							<!-- Will be populated by JS -->
						</svg>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						Each of the three weight matrices (<strong class="qkv-q-text">W<sub>Q</sub></strong>,
						<strong class="qkv-k-text">W<sub>K</sub></strong>,
						<strong class="qkv-v-text">W<sub>V</sub></strong>) has shape
						<code>[d<sub>model</sub> &times; d<sub>k</sub>]</code>.
						The multiplication projects each <code>d<sub>model</sub></code>-dimensional input down to a
						<code>d<sub>k</sub></code>-dimensional query, key, or value — a different <em>view</em>
						of the same token, optimized for a different purpose.
					</p>
					<p>
						These three projections happen in parallel for every token in the sequence.
						The result: three matrices — <strong class="qkv-q-text">Q</strong>,
						<strong class="qkv-k-text">K</strong>, and <strong class="qkv-v-text">V</strong> — each
						containing one row per token. Next, the queries and keys will be compared to compute
						<strong>attention scores</strong> — determining how much each token should attend to
						every other token.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* ===== Feature #9: Dot product attention + softmax ===== */}
				<div class="subsection-divider"></div>
				<h3 class="subsection-title"><span class="subsection-label">04b</span> Dot Product Attention &amp; Softmax</h3>

				<div class="linkage-callout linkage-attention">
					<span class="linkage-icon">&#8599;</span>
					<span>We now take the <strong class="qkv-q-text">Q</strong> and <strong class="qkv-k-text">K</strong> matrices from the previous step and use them to compute <strong>attention scores</strong> — how much each token should attend to every other token.</span>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						How does a token decide which other tokens are relevant? By comparing its
						<strong class="qkv-q-text">query</strong> against every other token’s
						<strong class="qkv-k-text">key</strong>. The comparison is a <strong>dot product</strong> —
						the simplest measure of how aligned two vectors are. If a query and key point in
						similar directions, their dot product is large (high attention). If they’re
						orthogonal, it’s near zero (no attention).
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Formula display */}
				<div class="attn-formula">
					<div class="attn-formula-row">
						Attention(<span class="qkv-q-text">Q</span>, <span class="qkv-k-text">K</span>, <span class="qkv-v-text">V</span>) = softmax(<span class="qkv-q-text">Q</span><span class="qkv-k-text">K</span><sup>T</sup> / &radic;<span class="attn-formula-dk">d<sub>k</sub></span>) <span class="qkv-v-text">V</span>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						Let’s break this down. For a sequence of three tokens, the dot product
						<strong class="qkv-q-text">Q</strong><strong class="qkv-k-text">K</strong><sup>T</sup>
						produces a 3×3 matrix of raw scores. Each cell (i, j) measures how much
						token i wants to attend to token j. But these raw scores can be huge, so we
						divide by &radic;d<sub>k</sub> to keep gradients stable. Then <strong>softmax</strong>
						converts each row into a probability distribution that sums to 1.
					</p>
					<p>
						Below, see how the raw dot-product scores for one token (“cat”) get transformed
						into attention weights. Use the <strong>temperature slider</strong> to see how
						sharpening (low temperature) makes the model focus on one token, while smoothing
						(high temperature) spreads attention evenly.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Interactive dot product + softmax visualization */}
				<div class="attn-dotprod-container">
					{/* Dot product grid: Q row × K columns */}
					<div class="attn-dotprod-grid">
						<div class="attn-grid-label">Raw scores for <span class="qkv-q-text">q<sub>2</sub></span> (“cat”) attending to each key:</div>
						<div class="attn-grid-row" id="attn-raw-scores">
							{/* Populated by JS */}
						</div>
					</div>

					{/* Scaling step */}
					<div class="attn-step-arrow">
						<span class="attn-step-label">&divide; &radic;d<sub>k</sub></span>
						<svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M12 5V19M12 19L6 13M12 19L18 13" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/></svg>
					</div>

					{/* Scaled scores */}
					<div class="attn-dotprod-grid">
						<div class="attn-grid-label">Scaled scores (/ &radic;d<sub>k</sub>):</div>
						<div class="attn-grid-row" id="attn-scaled-scores">
							{/* Populated by JS */}
						</div>
					</div>

					{/* Temperature slider */}
					<div class="attn-temp-control">
						<label class="attn-temp-label" id="attn-temp-display">Temperature: 1.0</label>
						<input type="range" id="attn-temp-slider" class="attn-temp-slider" min="0.1" max="3.0" step="0.1" value="1.0" />
						<div class="attn-temp-range">
							<span>0.1 (sharp)</span>
							<span>3.0 (smooth)</span>
						</div>
					</div>

					{/* Softmax step */}
					<div class="attn-step-arrow">
						<span class="attn-step-label">softmax</span>
						<svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M12 5V19M12 19L6 13M12 19L18 13" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/></svg>
					</div>

					{/* Softmax bar chart */}
					<div class="attn-softmax-chart">
						<div class="attn-grid-label">Attention weights (softmax output):</div>
						<div class="attn-bars-container" id="attn-bars">
							{/* Populated by JS */}
						</div>
						<div class="attn-bars-sum" id="attn-sum">Sum = 1.000</div>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						At temperature 1.0 (the default), “cat” attends most strongly to itself and
						to “sat” — tokens that are semantically and syntactically close. Lower the
						temperature toward 0.1 and the distribution becomes nearly one-hot: the model
						focuses on a single token. Raise it to 3.0 and attention spreads almost uniformly.
					</p>
					<p>
						The final step multiplies these attention weights by the
						<strong class="qkv-v-text">value</strong> vectors — producing a weighted
						sum that becomes the output of this attention head. Each token’s output is a
						blend of information from the tokens it attended to, with the attention weights
						controlling the mix.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* ===== Feature #10: Attention pattern heatmap ===== */}
				<div class="subsection-divider"></div>
				<h3 class="subsection-title"><span class="subsection-label">04c</span> Attention Pattern Heatmap</h3>

				<div class="linkage-callout linkage-attention">
					<span class="linkage-icon">&#8599;</span>
					<span>In the previous step, we computed attention weights for one query token. When we do this for <strong>every</strong> token in the sequence, the result is a full <strong>attention matrix</strong> — a square grid showing how much each token attends to every other.</span>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						The attention matrix is the heart of the transformer's context-gathering mechanism.
						Each row is one token's attention distribution (from the softmax we just saw), and each
						column shows how much that key token is attended to. The matrix is always square —
						<em>n</em> tokens produce an <em>n</em>&times;<em>n</em> grid.
					</p>
					<p>
						Hover over any cell below to see the exact attention weight and the source/target
						tokens. Notice the patterns: the model has learned which words are relevant to
						which — even in this short sentence.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="attn-heatmap-container" id="attn-heatmap">
					<div class="attn-heatmap-title">Attention weights for "The cat sat"</div>
					<div class="attn-heatmap-subtitle">Each row shows where that token looks (query → key)</div>

					{/* Single flat grid: 4 columns (label + 3 cells), 4 rows (header + 3 data) */}
					<div class="attn-heatmap-grid" id="heatmap-grid"></div>

					{/* Tooltip */}
					<div class="attn-heatmap-tooltip" id="heatmap-tooltip"></div>

					{/* Color scale legend */}
					<div class="attn-heatmap-legend">
						<span class="attn-heatmap-legend-label">0</span>
						<div class="attn-heatmap-legend-bar"></div>
						<span class="attn-heatmap-legend-label">1</span>
					</div>
					<div class="attn-heatmap-legend-caption">Attention weight (darker = stronger)</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						Notice the diagonal tendency — tokens often attend to themselves, which makes sense
						because a word's own context is usually relevant. But the off-diagonal cells are where
						the magic happens: "cat" attending strongly to "sat" captures the subject-verb
						relationship, while "sat" attending to "cat" lets the verb know its subject.
					</p>
					<p>
						This matrix is computed once per attention head. In a real transformer, there are
						multiple heads (typically 8&ndash;96), each learning to attend to different patterns —
						some capture syntax, some semantics, some positional relationships. We'll see how
						they combine in the next section.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* ===== Feature #11: Value-weighted output animation ===== */}
				<div class="subsection-divider"></div>
				<h3 class="subsection-title"><span class="subsection-label">04d</span> Value-Weighted Output</h3>

				<div class="linkage-callout linkage-attention">
					<span class="linkage-icon">&#8599;</span>
					<span>Now we combine the <strong class="qkv-v-text">value vectors</strong> from step 04a with the <strong>attention weights</strong> from the heatmap — producing the final output of one attention head.</span>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						Each token's output is a <strong>weighted sum</strong> of all value vectors, where the weights
						come from the attention scores. A high attention weight means "pull in a lot of
						information from this token"; a low weight means "mostly ignore it."
					</p>
					<p>
						Let's compute the output for "cat" (token 2). Its attention weights are
						<strong>0.21</strong> for "The", <strong>0.46</strong> for "cat", and <strong>0.33</strong> for "sat".
						Watch how each value vector gets scaled by its weight and then summed:
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="val-output-container" id="val-output">
					<div class="val-output-title">Computing output for "cat"</div>

					{/* Step-by-step weighted sum */}
					<div class="val-output-steps" id="val-output-steps">
						{/* Row 1: weight × V for "The" */}
						<div class="val-step-row">
							<div class="val-weight" style="opacity: 0.55;">0.21</div>
							<div class="val-times">&times;</div>
