						<span>04b</span>
						<span></span>
						<span>04b</span>
						<span></span>
						<span>04d</span>
						<span></span>
						<span>04d</span>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						That's the complete single-head attention pipeline. Every token goes through
						this process in parallel, producing context-enriched output vectors. But one
						attention head can only capture one type of relationship. In the next section,
						we'll see what happens when we run <strong>multiple heads in parallel</strong> —
						each learning to focus on different aspects of the input.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>


			{/* ===== Section 05: Multi-Head Attention ===== */}
			<section id="multi-head-attention" class="mb-24 scroll-mt-20">
				<div class="mb-8 flex items-baseline gap-4">
					<span class="font-mono text-sm text-accent/60">05</span>
					<div>
						<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-accent-2 sm:text-3xl">Multi-Head Attention</h2>
						<p class="mt-1 text-base text-textColor/60">Many perspectives, one representation</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="linkage-callout linkage-attention">
					<span class="linkage-icon">&#8599;</span>
					<span>In the previous section, we built a <strong>single attention head</strong> &mdash; a complete pipeline from input vectors to context-enriched outputs. Now we&rsquo;ll see why one head isn&rsquo;t enough, and what happens when we run <strong>several heads in parallel</strong>.</span>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						A single attention head can only learn <strong>one type of relationship</strong> at a time.
						It might learn to connect subjects to verbs, or to link pronouns to their antecedents &mdash;
						but it can&rsquo;t do both simultaneously. Language is far too rich for a single perspective.
					</p>
					<p>
						The solution is <strong>multi-head attention</strong>: run multiple attention heads in parallel,
						each with its own learned W<sub>Q</sub>, W<sub>K</sub>, W<sub>V</sub> weight matrices.
						Each head independently decides what to attend to, learning to capture different
						aspects of the input. Think of it like looking at a sentence from multiple angles
						at once &mdash; one head for grammar, another for meaning, another for proximity.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Parallel heads diagram: single head duplicated */}
				<div class="mha-parallel-intro">
					<div class="mha-parallel-title">One head becomes many</div>
					<div class="mha-parallel-diagram">
						<div class="mha-single-head-box">
							<div class="mha-head-label">Single head</div>
							<div class="mha-head-pipeline">
								<span class="mha-mini-box mha-mini-qkv"><span class="qkv-q-text">Q</span><span class="qkv-k-text">K</span><span class="qkv-v-text">V</span></span>
								<span class="mha-mini-arrow">&rarr;</span>
								<span class="mha-mini-box mha-mini-attn">Attn</span>
								<span class="mha-mini-arrow">&rarr;</span>
								<span class="mha-mini-box mha-mini-out">Out</span>
							</div>
						</div>
						<div class="mha-duplicate-arrow">&darr; &times;h</div>
						<div class="mha-multi-head-stack">
							<div class="mha-head-slot mha-head-1">
								<span class="mha-head-tag">Head 1</span>
								<span class="mha-mini-box mha-mini-qkv"><span class="qkv-q-text">Q</span><span class="qkv-k-text">K</span><span class="qkv-v-text">V</span></span>
								<span class="mha-mini-arrow">&rarr;</span>
								<span class="mha-mini-box mha-mini-attn">Attn</span>
								<span class="mha-mini-arrow">&rarr;</span>
								<span class="mha-mini-box mha-mini-out">Out</span>
							</div>
							<div class="mha-head-slot mha-head-2">
								<span class="mha-head-tag">Head 2</span>
								<span class="mha-mini-box mha-mini-qkv"><span class="qkv-q-text">Q</span><span class="qkv-k-text">K</span><span class="qkv-v-text">V</span></span>
								<span class="mha-mini-arrow">&rarr;</span>
								<span class="mha-mini-box mha-mini-attn">Attn</span>
								<span class="mha-mini-arrow">&rarr;</span>
								<span class="mha-mini-box mha-mini-out">Out</span>
							</div>
							<div class="mha-head-slot mha-head-3">
								<span class="mha-head-tag">Head 3</span>
								<span class="mha-mini-box mha-mini-qkv"><span class="qkv-q-text">Q</span><span class="qkv-k-text">K</span><span class="qkv-v-text">V</span></span>
								<span class="mha-mini-arrow">&rarr;</span>
								<span class="mha-mini-box mha-mini-attn">Attn</span>
								<span class="mha-mini-arrow">&rarr;</span>
								<span class="mha-mini-box mha-mini-out">Out</span>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						Each head receives the <strong>same input vectors</strong> but learns completely
						different weight matrices. This means each head computes its own Q, K, V
						projections and its own attention pattern. The result is that different heads
						specialize in different tasks &mdash; without anyone telling them to. The
						specialization emerges naturally during training.
					</p>
					<p>
						Below are three example attention patterns for our familiar sentence &ldquo;The cat sat.&rdquo;
						Notice how each head learns to focus on completely different relationships:
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Three head heatmaps side by side */}
				<div class="mha-heads-gallery">
					<div class="mha-head-card" id="mha-head-card-1">
						<div class="mha-head-card-title">
							<span class="mha-head-number">Head 1</span>
							<span class="mha-head-role">Positional / adjacency</span>
						</div>
						<div class="mha-head-card-desc">Attends to nearby tokens &mdash; especially the immediately preceding word.</div>
						<div class="mha-heatmap-grid" id="mha-heatmap-1"></div>
					</div>
					<div class="mha-head-card" id="mha-head-card-2">
						<div class="mha-head-card-title">
							<span class="mha-head-number">Head 2</span>
							<span class="mha-head-role">Syntactic / subject-verb</span>
						</div>
						<div class="mha-head-card-desc">Connects subjects to their verbs &mdash; &ldquo;cat&rdquo; strongly attends to &ldquo;sat.&rdquo;</div>
						<div class="mha-heatmap-grid" id="mha-heatmap-2"></div>
					</div>
					<div class="mha-head-card" id="mha-head-card-3">
						<div class="mha-head-card-title">
							<span class="mha-head-number">Head 3</span>
							<span class="mha-head-role">Self-identity / broad context</span>
						</div>
						<div class="mha-head-card-desc">Each token attends mostly to itself, preserving its own information.</div>
						<div class="mha-heatmap-grid" id="mha-heatmap-3"></div>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						Head 1 acts like a <strong>positional head</strong> &mdash; it focuses on adjacent tokens,
						helping each word understand its immediate context. Head 2 is a <strong>syntactic head</strong> &mdash;
						it links grammatically related words like subjects and verbs, even across distance.
						Head 3 is a <strong>self-identity head</strong> &mdash; it lets each token preserve its own
						representation while blending in a little context from everything else.
					</p>
					<p>
						In real transformers, this effect scales dramatically. GPT-2 has 12 heads per layer;
						GPT-4 and Claude likely have 64&ndash;96+. Researchers have discovered heads that specialize
						in <strong>induction</strong> (copying patterns), <strong>previous-token</strong> (always looking one step back),
						<strong>duplicate-token</strong> (finding repeated words), and many more. The model figures out
						which perspectives are useful entirely on its own.
					</p>
					<p>
						But how do these different perspectives get combined into a single output? That&rsquo;s
						the next step: <strong>concatenation and projection</strong>.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* ===== Feature #13: Concatenation & Projection ===== */}
				<div class="subsection-divider"></div>
				<h3 class="subsection-title"><span class="subsection-number">05b</span> Concatenation &amp; Projection</h3>

				<div class="linkage-callout linkage-attention">
					<span class="linkage-icon">&#8599;</span>
					<span>The three heads above each produced their own output vectors. Now we <strong>concatenate</strong> them into one wide vector and <strong>project</strong> it back to the model dimension with W<sub>O</sub>.</span>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						Each attention head operates on a <strong>subspace</strong> of the full model dimension.
						If d<sub>model</sub> = 768 and we have h = 12 heads, each head works with
						d<sub>k</sub> = 768/12 = 64 dimensions. This is critical: multi-head attention doesn&rsquo;t
						cost more compute than a single large head &mdash; it just <strong>divides the dimensions</strong>
						among multiple perspectives.
					</p>
					<p>
						After all heads run in parallel, their outputs are concatenated back into a single
						vector of the original d<sub>model</sub> size, then multiplied by one final weight matrix
						W<sub>O</sub> to mix the heads&rsquo; contributions together.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Formula display */}
				<div class="mha-formula">
					MultiHead(Q, K, V) = Concat(head<sub>1</sub>, &hellip;, head<sub>h</sub>) &middot; W<sub>O</sub>
				</div>

				{/* Concatenation + projection diagram */}
				<div class="mha-concat-diagram" id="mha-concat-diagram">
					<div class="mha-concat-title">Concatenation &amp; projection</div>

					{/* Head output vectors */}
					<div class="mha-concat-heads" id="mha-concat-heads">
						<div class="mha-concat-head-vec mha-concat-h1" data-head="1">
							<div class="mha-concat-head-label">Head 1</div>
							<div class="mha-concat-head-vals">[0.12, 0.45, &hellip;]</div>
							<div class="mha-concat-head-dim">d<sub>k</sub>=64</div>
						</div>
						<div class="mha-concat-head-vec mha-concat-h2" data-head="2">
							<div class="mha-concat-head-label">Head 2</div>
							<div class="mha-concat-head-vals">[&minus;0.31, 0.67, &hellip;]</div>
							<div class="mha-concat-head-dim">d<sub>k</sub>=64</div>
						</div>
						<div class="mha-concat-head-vec mha-concat-h3" data-head="3">
							<div class="mha-concat-head-label">Head 3</div>
							<div class="mha-concat-head-vals">[0.58, &minus;0.14, &hellip;]</div>
							<div class="mha-concat-head-dim">d<sub>k</sub>=64</div>
						</div>
					</div>

					<div class="mha-concat-arrow">&darr; Concat</div>

					{/* Concatenated wide vector */}
					<div class="mha-concat-wide" id="mha-concat-wide">
						<div class="mha-concat-wide-label">Concatenated</div>
						<div class="mha-concat-wide-bar">
							<div class="mha-concat-wide-seg mha-seg-h1" data-head="1">H1</div>
							<div class="mha-concat-wide-seg mha-seg-h2" data-head="2">H2</div>
							<div class="mha-concat-wide-seg mha-seg-h3" data-head="3">H3</div>
						</div>
						<div class="mha-concat-wide-dim">h &times; d<sub>k</sub> = 3 &times; 64 = 192</div>
					</div>

					<div class="mha-concat-arrow">&darr; &times; W<sub>O</sub></div>

					{/* Projected output vector */}
					<div class="mha-concat-output" id="mha-concat-output">
						<div class="mha-concat-output-label">Multi-Head Output</div>
						<div class="mha-concat-output-vals">[0.23, &minus;0.08, 0.41, 0.55, &hellip;]</div>
						<div class="mha-concat-output-dim">d<sub>model</sub> = 768</div>
					</div>
				</div>

				{/* Interactive head toggle */}
				<div class="mha-toggle-container" id="mha-toggle-container">
					<div class="mha-toggle-title">Toggle heads on/off</div>
					<div class="mha-toggle-desc">Disable a head to see how the output changes. Each head contributes a unique perspective.</div>
					<div class="mha-toggle-buttons" id="mha-toggle-buttons">
						<button class="mha-toggle-btn mha-toggle-active" data-head="1" id="mha-toggle-1">
							<span class="mha-toggle-indicator"></span>
							Head 1 <span class="mha-toggle-role">positional</span>
						</button>
						<button class="mha-toggle-btn mha-toggle-active" data-head="2" id="mha-toggle-2">
							<span class="mha-toggle-indicator"></span>
							Head 2 <span class="mha-toggle-role">syntactic</span>
						</button>
						<button class="mha-toggle-btn mha-toggle-active" data-head="3" id="mha-toggle-3">
							<span class="mha-toggle-indicator"></span>
							Head 3 <span class="mha-toggle-role">identity</span>
						</button>
					</div>
					<div class="mha-toggle-result" id="mha-toggle-result">
						<div class="mha-toggle-result-label">Combined output for &ldquo;cat&rdquo;:</div>
						<div class="mha-toggle-result-vec" id="mha-toggle-result-vec">[0.23, &minus;0.08, 0.41, 0.55, &hellip;]</div>
						<div class="mha-toggle-result-note" id="mha-toggle-result-note">All 3 heads active &mdash; full context from position, syntax, and identity.</div>
					</div>
				</div>

				{/* Closing prose + residual stream handoff */}
				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						The output of multi-head attention is a vector of the <strong>same dimension</strong> as the input
						(d<sub>model</sub>). This is by design: the output needs to be <strong>added back</strong> to the original
						input via a residual connection. The attention output doesn&rsquo;t replace the input &mdash;
						it <strong>enriches</strong> it with contextual information from other tokens.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Residual stream handoff diagram */}
				<div class="mha-residual-handoff">
					<div class="mha-residual-title">Adding back to the residual stream</div>
					<div class="mha-residual-flow">
						<div class="mha-residual-box mha-residual-input">
							<div class="mha-residual-box-label">Input</div>
							<div class="mha-residual-box-val">x</div>
						</div>
						<div class="mha-residual-plus">+</div>
						<div class="mha-residual-box mha-residual-attn">
							<div class="mha-residual-box-label">Multi-Head Attn</div>
							<div class="mha-residual-box-val">MHA(x)</div>
						</div>
						<div class="mha-residual-equals">=</div>
						<div class="mha-residual-box mha-residual-result">
							<div class="mha-residual-box-label">Enriched</div>
							<div class="mha-residual-box-val">x + MHA(x)</div>
						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="mha-residual-caption">This enriched vector then flows into the feed-forward network &mdash; the next stop in the transformer layer.</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						That completes the multi-head attention mechanism. Each token&rsquo;s representation
						has been enriched with context from every other token, viewed through multiple
						independent perspectives. Next, the enriched vectors pass through the
						<strong>feed-forward network</strong> &mdash; where per-token computation and memory storage happen.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>


			{/* ===== Section 06: Feed-Forward Network ===== */}
			<section id="ffn" class="mb-24 scroll-mt-20">
				<div class="mb-8 flex items-baseline gap-4">
					<span class="font-mono text-sm text-accent/60">06</span>
					<div>
						<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-accent-2 sm:text-3xl">Feed-Forward Network</h2>
						<p class="mt-1 text-base text-textColor/60">Per-token computation and memory</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="linkage-callout linkage-ffn">
					<span class="linkage-icon">&#8599;</span>
					<span>We&rsquo;re now in the <strong>Feed-Forward Network</strong> block &mdash; the second half of each transformer layer. The enriched vectors from multi-head attention (x + MHA(x)) arrive here for per-token processing.</span>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						After attention has mixed information across tokens, each token passes independently
						through a <strong>feed-forward network</strong> (FFN). Unlike attention, which lets tokens
						communicate, the FFN processes each token in isolation &mdash; the same weights
						applied to every position. Think of attention as a <strong>meeting</strong> where tokens
						exchange information, and the FFN as <strong>individual work time</strong> where each token
						processes what it learned.
					</p>
					<p>
						The FFN has a distinctive two-layer structure: it <strong>expands</strong> the vector to a
						higher dimension, applies a nonlinear activation function, then <strong>compresses</strong>
						it back. This expand-activate-compress pattern is where much of the transformer&rsquo;s
						&ldquo;knowledge&rdquo; is stored.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* FFN structure diagram: expand-activate-compress */}
				<div class="ffn-structure-diagram">
					<div class="ffn-structure-title">Feed-forward network structure</div>
					<div class="ffn-structure-formula">FFN(x) = W<sub>2</sub> &middot; &sigma;(W<sub>1</sub> &middot; x + b<sub>1</sub>) + b<sub>2</sub></div>
					<div class="ffn-bulge">
						<div class="ffn-bulge-stage ffn-stage-input">
							<div class="ffn-bar" style="height: 60px;"></div>
							<div class="ffn-stage-label">Input</div>
							<div class="ffn-stage-dim">d<sub>model</sub><br>768</div>
						</div>
						<div class="ffn-bulge-arrow">&rarr;</div>
						<div class="ffn-bulge-stage ffn-stage-w1">
							<div class="ffn-bar-label">W<sub>1</sub></div>
							<div class="ffn-bar" style="height: 30px;"></div>
						</div>
						<div class="ffn-bulge-arrow">&rarr;</div>
						<div class="ffn-bulge-stage ffn-stage-expanded">
							<div class="ffn-bar ffn-bar-tall" style="height: 180px;"></div>
							<div class="ffn-stage-label">&sigma; Activation</div>
							<div class="ffn-stage-dim">4 &times; d<sub>model</sub><br>3072</div>
						</div>
						<div class="ffn-bulge-arrow">&rarr;</div>
						<div class="ffn-bulge-stage ffn-stage-w2">
							<div class="ffn-bar-label">W<sub>2</sub></div>
							<div class="ffn-bar" style="height: 30px;"></div>
						</div>
						<div class="ffn-bulge-arrow">&rarr;</div>
						<div class="ffn-bulge-stage ffn-stage-output">
							<div class="ffn-bar" style="height: 60px;"></div>
							<div class="ffn-stage-label">Output</div>
							<div class="ffn-stage-dim">d<sub>model</sub><br>768</div>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						The expansion ratio is typically <strong>4&times;</strong>: a 768-dimensional input becomes
						a 3072-dimensional hidden representation, then gets compressed back to 768.
						This &ldquo;bulge&rdquo; creates a much larger space for the network to work in &mdash;
						like expanding into a bigger workshop, doing detailed work, then packing
						the results back into a compact form.
					</p>
					<p>
						The nonlinear <strong>activation function</strong> between the two linear layers is what
						gives the FFN its power. Without it, stacking two linear transformations would
						just be one bigger linear transformation &mdash; nothing interesting.
						The activation introduces the <strong>nonlinearity</strong> that lets the network learn
						complex patterns.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Interactive activation function comparison */}
				<div class="ffn-activation-container" id="ffn-activation">
					<div class="ffn-activation-title">Activation functions</div>
					<div class="ffn-activation-buttons" id="ffn-activation-buttons">
						<button class="ffn-act-btn ffn-act-active" data-fn="relu" id="ffn-btn-relu">ReLU</button>
						<button class="ffn-act-btn" data-fn="gelu" id="ffn-btn-gelu">GELU</button>
						<button class="ffn-act-btn" data-fn="silu" id="ffn-btn-silu">SiLU / Swish</button>
					</div>
					<canvas id="ffn-activation-canvas" width="480" height="280"></canvas>
					<div class="ffn-activation-desc" id="ffn-activation-desc">
						<strong>ReLU</strong>: max(0, x). The original transformer used this. Simple and fast, but
						&ldquo;dead neurons&rdquo; (always zero for negative inputs) can waste capacity.
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						Researchers have found that the FFN layers act as a kind of <strong>key-value memory</strong>.
						Each neuron in the expanded layer acts like a &ldquo;pattern detector&rdquo; &mdash; it activates
						when it recognizes certain features in the input. The first linear layer (W<sub>1</sub>) selects
						which patterns match, the activation function gates them, and the second linear layer
						(W<sub>2</sub>) maps the activated patterns back to the output space. This is where much of the
						transformer&rsquo;s factual knowledge lives.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Residual connection: FFN output added back */}
				<div class="ffn-residual-handoff">
					<div class="ffn-residual-title">Completing one transformer layer</div>
					<div class="ffn-residual-flow">
						<div class="ffn-residual-box ffn-res-input">
							<div class="ffn-residual-box-label">After Attention</div>
							<div class="ffn-residual-box-val">x + MHA(x)</div>
						</div>
						<div class="ffn-residual-plus">+</div>
						<div class="ffn-residual-box ffn-res-ffn">
							<div class="ffn-residual-box-label">FFN Output</div>
							<div class="ffn-residual-box-val">FFN(&hellip;)</div>
						</div>
						<div class="ffn-residual-equals">=</div>
						<div class="ffn-residual-box ffn-res-result">
							<div class="ffn-residual-box-label">Layer Output</div>
							<div class="ffn-residual-box-val">x&prime;</div>
						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="ffn-residual-caption">This completes one transformer layer. The output x&prime; then flows into the next layer &mdash; through another attention + FFN cycle &mdash; or, if this is the final layer, into the output head.</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						That&rsquo;s one complete transformer layer: <strong>multi-head attention</strong> for cross-token
						communication, then a <strong>feed-forward network</strong> for per-token processing, both
						connected through <strong>residual connections</strong>. In a typical model, this pattern
						repeats 12&ndash;96 times. The residual stream &mdash; the highway that carries
						information through all these layers &mdash; is the subject of the next section.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>


			<section
				id="residual-stream"
				class="mb-24 scroll-mt-20"
			>
				<div class="mb-8 flex items-baseline gap-4">
					<span class="font-mono text-sm text-accent/60">07</span>
					<div>
						<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-accent-2 sm:text-3xl">Residual Stream</h2>
						<p class="mt-1 text-base text-textColor/60">The information highway through the network</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="linkage-callout linkage-residual">
					<span class="linkage-icon">&#8599;</span>
					<span>We&rsquo;ve now seen both halves of a single transformer layer: <strong>multi-head attention</strong> for cross-token communication and the <strong>feed-forward network</strong> for per-token processing. But what connects them &mdash; and what lets information flow through dozens of these layers? The <strong>residual stream</strong>.</span>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						Imagine a <strong>highway</strong> that runs through the entire transformer. At each layer,
						attention and FFN blocks are like <strong>on-ramps and off-ramps</strong> &mdash; they read
						from the highway, do their computation, and <strong>add</strong> their output back. The
						original signal is never destroyed; it just accumulates new information at each stop.
					</p>
					<p>
						This is the <strong>residual connection</strong> &mdash; a deceptively simple idea
						(just add the input to the output) that turns out to be critical. Without it,
						gradients vanish as they backpropagate through dozens of layers, making deep
						networks impossible to train. With it, the gradient has a &ldquo;shortcut&rdquo; that
						bypasses every layer, keeping the signal strong.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Residual stream highway diagram */}
				<div class="res-highway-diagram">
					<div class="res-highway-title">The residual stream through one layer</div>
					<div class="res-highway-track">
						<div class="res-highway-stream" id="res-highway-stream">
							<div class="res-stream-segment res-seg-input">
								<div class="res-stream-label">x</div>
							</div>
							<div class="res-stream-junction">
								<div class="res-junction-branch res-branch-attn">
									<div class="res-branch-arrow">&uarr;</div>
									<div class="res-branch-block res-block-attn">
										<div class="res-block-name">Multi-Head<br>Attention</div>
									</div>
									<div class="res-branch-arrow">&darr;</div>
								</div>
								<div class="res-junction-plus">+</div>
							</div>
							<div class="res-stream-segment res-seg-mid">
								<div class="res-stream-label">x + MHA(x)</div>
							</div>
							<div class="res-stream-junction">
								<div class="res-junction-branch res-branch-ffn">
									<div class="res-branch-arrow">&uarr;</div>
									<div class="res-branch-block res-block-ffn">
										<div class="res-block-name">Feed-Forward<br>Network</div>
									</div>
									<div class="res-branch-arrow">&darr;</div>
								</div>
								<div class="res-junction-plus">+</div>
							</div>
							<div class="res-stream-segment res-seg-output">
								<div class="res-stream-label">x&prime;</div>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="res-highway-caption">The purple highway is the residual stream. Attention (amber) and FFN (green) read from it, compute, and add their results back. The original signal x flows straight through.</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						Here&rsquo;s the key insight: each sublayer (attention or FFN) computes a <strong>delta</strong>
						&mdash; a change to apply &mdash; rather than replacing the stream. This means every
						layer has access to information from <em>all</em> previous layers, not just the one before it.
						The residual stream is a <strong>shared workspace</strong> that every component can read from
						and write to.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Animated residual accumulation across layers */}
				<div class="res-layers-diagram">
					<div class="res-layers-title">Stacking layers: the residual stream accumulates</div>
					<div class="res-layers-controls">
						<button class="res-layers-btn" id="res-animate-btn">
							<svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor"><polygon points="5,3 19,12 5,21"/></svg>
							Animate
						</button>
						<button class="res-layers-btn" id="res-reset-btn">
							<svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M1 4v6h6M23 20v-6h-6"/><path d="M20.49 9A9 9 0 005.64 5.64L1 10m22 4l-4.64 4.36A9 9 0 013.51 15"/></svg>
							Reset
						</button>
					</div>
					<div class="res-layers-stack" id="res-layers-stack">
						<div class="res-layer-row" data-layer="0">
							<div class="res-layer-label">Input</div>
							<div class="res-layer-bar-container">
								<div class="res-layer-bar res-bar-embed" style="width: 100%;">
									<span>Embedding</span>
								</div>
							</div>
						</div>
						<div class="res-layer-row" data-layer="1">
							<div class="res-layer-label">Layer 1</div>
							<div class="res-layer-bar-container">
								<div class="res-layer-bar res-bar-embed" style="width: 55%;"><span>Emb</span></div>
								<div class="res-layer-bar res-bar-attn" style="width: 25%;"><span>Attn 1</span></div>
								<div class="res-layer-bar res-bar-ffn" style="width: 20%;"><span>FFN 1</span></div>
							</div>
						</div>
						<div class="res-layer-row" data-layer="2">
							<div class="res-layer-label">Layer 2</div>
							<div class="res-layer-bar-container">
								<div class="res-layer-bar res-bar-embed" style="width: 35%;"><span>Emb</span></div>
								<div class="res-layer-bar res-bar-attn" style="width: 20%;"><span>A1</span></div>
								<div class="res-layer-bar res-bar-ffn" style="width: 13%;"><span>F1</span></div>
								<div class="res-layer-bar res-bar-attn" style="width: 18%;"><span>Attn 2</span></div>
								<div class="res-layer-bar res-bar-ffn" style="width: 14%;"><span>FFN 2</span></div>
							</div>
						</div>
						<div class="res-layer-row" data-layer="3">
							<div class="res-layer-label">Layer 3</div>
							<div class="res-layer-bar-container">
								<div class="res-layer-bar res-bar-embed" style="width: 22%;"><span>Emb</span></div>
								<div class="res-layer-bar res-bar-attn" style="width: 13%;"><span>A1</span></div>
								<div class="res-layer-bar res-bar-ffn" style="width: 8%;"><span>F1</span></div>
								<div class="res-layer-bar res-bar-attn" style="width: 14%;"><span>A2</span></div>
								<div class="res-layer-bar res-bar-ffn" style="width: 10%;"><span>F2</span></div>
								<div class="res-layer-bar res-bar-attn" style="width: 18%;"><span>Attn 3</span></div>
								<div class="res-layer-bar res-bar-ffn" style="width: 15%;"><span>FFN 3</span></div>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="res-layers-legend">
						<span class="res-legend-item"><span class="res-legend-swatch res-swatch-embed"></span>Embedding</span>
						<span class="res-legend-item"><span class="res-legend-swatch res-swatch-attn"></span>Attention</span>
						<span class="res-legend-item"><span class="res-legend-swatch res-swatch-ffn"></span>FFN</span>
					</div>
					<div class="res-layers-caption">Each row shows the composition of the residual stream at that point. As layers accumulate, the stream becomes a rich mix of contributions from embedding, attention, and FFN blocks.</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						But there&rsquo;s a problem. As values flow through dozens of addition operations, they
						can grow uncontrollably large &mdash; or different dimensions can drift to wildly
						different scales. Training becomes unstable. This is where <strong>layer normalization</strong>
						comes in.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Layer normalization interactive */}
				<h3 class="subsection-divider" id="layer-norm">
					<span class="subsection-number">07b</span>
					<span class="subsection-title">Layer Normalization</span>
				</h3>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						Layer normalization is a <strong>normalization gate</strong> that sits before each sublayer
						(in the &ldquo;Pre-Norm&rdquo; variant used by most modern transformers). It rescales each
						vector so the values across dimensions have <strong>zero mean</strong> and <strong>unit variance</strong>,
						then applies learned scale (&gamma;) and shift (&beta;) parameters.
					</p>
					<p>
						The effect is simple but powerful: no matter how large or skewed the values
						in the residual stream become, layer norm brings them back to a stable range
						before each sublayer processes them. Think of it as a <strong>reset button for scale</strong>
						&mdash; it preserves the direction (meaning) of the vector while standardizing its magnitude.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="ln-interactive" id="ln-interactive">
					<div class="ln-title">Layer normalization in action</div>
					<div class="ln-formula">LayerNorm(x) = &gamma; &middot; (x &minus; &mu;) / &sigma; + &beta;</div>
					<div class="ln-columns">
						<div class="ln-column">
							<div class="ln-col-title">Before normalization</div>
							<div class="ln-bars" id="ln-before-bars"></div>
							<div class="ln-stats" id="ln-before-stats"></div>
						</div>
						<div class="ln-arrow">&rarr;</div>
						<div class="ln-column">
							<div class="ln-col-title">After normalization</div>
							<div class="ln-bars" id="ln-after-bars"></div>
							<div class="ln-stats" id="ln-after-stats"></div>
						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="ln-randomize">
						<button class="ln-randomize-btn" id="ln-randomize-btn">Randomize values</button>
					</div>
					<div class="ln-caption">Click &ldquo;Randomize&rdquo; to see how layer norm handles different input distributions. The output always has mean &asymp; 0 and std &asymp; 1, regardless of the input scale.</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						Modern transformers (GPT-2 onward) typically use <strong>Pre-Norm</strong> ordering: layer norm
						is applied <em>before</em> each sublayer, not after. This makes training more stable because
						each sublayer receives well-conditioned inputs. The pattern for each layer is:
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Pre-norm ordering diagram */}
				<div class="res-prenorm-diagram">
					<div class="res-prenorm-title">Pre-Norm layer ordering (modern transformers)</div>
					<div class="res-prenorm-flow">
						<div class="res-prenorm-step res-pn-stream">
							<div class="res-pn-label">Residual</div>
							<div class="res-pn-val">x</div>
						</div>
						<div class="res-prenorm-arrow">&rarr;</div>
						<div class="res-prenorm-step res-pn-norm">
							<div class="res-pn-label">Layer Norm</div>
						</div>
						<div class="res-prenorm-arrow">&rarr;</div>
						<div class="res-prenorm-step res-pn-attn">
							<div class="res-pn-label">MHA</div>
						</div>
						<div class="res-prenorm-arrow">&rarr;</div>
						<div class="res-prenorm-step res-pn-add">
							<div class="res-pn-label">+ x</div>
						</div>
						<div class="res-prenorm-arrow">&rarr;</div>
						<div class="res-prenorm-step res-pn-norm">
							<div class="res-pn-label">Layer Norm</div>
						</div>
						<div class="res-prenorm-arrow">&rarr;</div>
						<div class="res-prenorm-step res-pn-ffn">
							<div class="res-pn-label">FFN</div>
						</div>
						<div class="res-prenorm-arrow">&rarr;</div>
						<div class="res-prenorm-step res-pn-add">
							<div class="res-pn-label">+ x</div>
						</div>
						<div class="res-prenorm-arrow">&rarr;</div>
						<div class="res-prenorm-step res-pn-stream">
							<div class="res-pn-label">Residual</div>
							<div class="res-pn-val">x&prime;</div>
						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="res-prenorm-caption">In Pre-Norm, layer normalization comes <em>before</em> each sublayer. The &ldquo;+ x&rdquo; nodes are the residual additions, feeding back into the stream.</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						Finally, let&rsquo;s see how stacking these layers creates the full transformer.
						A typical model has <strong>12&ndash;96 layers</strong>, each running the same
						attention &rarr; residual &rarr; FFN &rarr; residual cycle. The residual stream flows through
						all of them, accumulating increasingly abstract representations.
					</p>
					<p>
						Early layers tend to learn <strong>local, syntactic patterns</strong> (like part-of-speech tagging),
						while later layers capture <strong>abstract, semantic relationships</strong> (like factual knowledge
						and reasoning). The residual stream is what makes this gradual refinement possible &mdash;
						each layer can read what previous layers wrote and build on it, rather than starting from scratch.
					</p>
					<p>
						The residual stream also connects back to the architecture diagram from section 02 &mdash;
						it&rsquo;s the vertical highway labeled &ldquo;+ residual&rdquo; that runs through the &ldquo;&times;N layers&rdquo;
						block. Now that we understand what happens inside each layer and how they connect,
						the next step is to see what happens at the very end: turning the final residual vector
						back into a word prediction.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>


			<section id="output" class="mb-24 scroll-mt-20">
				<div class="mb-8 flex items-baseline gap-4">
					<span class="font-mono text-sm text-accent/60">08</span>
					<div>
						<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-accent-2 sm:text-3xl">Output</h2>
						<p class="mt-1 text-base text-textColor/60">From vectors back to words</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="linkage-callout linkage-output">
					<span class="linkage-icon">&nearr;</span>
					<span>The vector has traveled through all N layers of attention and feed-forward processing. It now carries a rich, abstract representation of the token in context. We&rsquo;re at the <strong>Unembedding</strong> block at the very top of the architecture diagram &mdash; the final step before a prediction emerges.</span>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						At the start of this explainer, we turned words into vectors &mdash; each token became a point in
						a high-dimensional space, carrying meaning and position. Now we need to do the <strong>reverse</strong>:
						take the final vector from the residual stream and turn it back into a word prediction.
					</p>
					<p>
						This is the job of the <strong>unembedding layer</strong>. It takes the d<sub>model</sub>-dimensional
						vector and projects it to a vector of size <strong>|V|</strong> (the vocabulary size &mdash; typically
						32k&ndash;100k). Each entry in this vector is a <strong>logit</strong>: a raw, unnormalized score
						for how likely that token is to come next.
					</p>
				</div>

				<!-- Embedding ↔ Unembedding symmetry diagram -->
				<div class="out-symmetry-diagram">
					<div class="out-symmetry-title">Embedding &harr; Unembedding: mirror operations</div>
					<div class="out-symmetry-flow">
						<div class="out-sym-col">
							<div class="out-sym-box out-sym-embed">
								<div class="out-sym-label">Embedding (Section 03)</div>
								<div class="out-sym-desc">token &rarr; vector</div>
								<div class="out-sym-shape">W<sub>E</sub>: |V| &times; d<sub>model</sub></div>
							</div>
							<div class="out-sym-example">
								<span class="token-chip token-whole">cat</span>
								<span class="out-sym-arrow">&rarr;</span>
								<span class="out-sym-vec">[0.23, &minus;0.41, &hellip;]</span>
							</div>
						</div>
						<div class="out-sym-mirror">
							<div class="out-sym-mirror-line"></div>
							<div class="out-sym-mirror-label">&harr;</div>
							<div class="out-sym-mirror-line"></div>
						</div>
						<div class="out-sym-col">
							<div class="out-sym-box out-sym-unembed">
								<div class="out-sym-label">Unembedding (This section)</div>
								<div class="out-sym-desc">vector &rarr; logits</div>
								<div class="out-sym-shape">W<sub>U</sub>: d<sub>model</sub> &times; |V|</div>
							</div>
							<div class="out-sym-example">
								<span class="out-sym-vec">[&hellip;, 0.55, &minus;0.08]</span>
								<span class="out-sym-arrow">&rarr;</span>
								<span class="out-sym-logits">logits<sub>50k</sub></span>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="out-symmetry-caption">
						The embedding matrix W<sub>E</sub> maps tokens to vectors; the unembedding matrix W<sub>U</sub> maps vectors back to vocabulary-sized scores. In many models, W<sub>U</sub> &asymp; W<sub>E</sub><sup>T</sup> (weight tying).
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						The logits are raw scores &mdash; they can be any real number, positive or negative.
						To turn them into a proper probability distribution, we apply <strong>softmax</strong>:
						each logit is exponentiated and then divided by the sum of all exponentiated logits.
						The result is a probability for every token in the vocabulary, summing to 1.
					</p>
					<p>
						But before applying softmax, we can introduce a <strong>temperature</strong> parameter
						that controls how &ldquo;sharp&rdquo; or &ldquo;flat&rdquo; the distribution is. Low temperature
						(&lt; 1) makes the model more confident &mdash; it concentrates probability on the top
						choices. High temperature (&gt; 1) makes the model more creative &mdash; it spreads
						probability more evenly across alternatives.
					</p>
				</div>

				<!-- Output prediction interactive -->
				<div class="out-prediction-widget">
					<div class="out-prediction-title">Next-token prediction</div>
					<div class="out-prediction-context">
						<span class="out-context-label">Context:</span>
						<span class="out-context-tokens">
							<span class="token-chip token-whole">The</span>
							<span class="token-chip token-whole">cat</span>
							<span class="token-chip token-whole">sat</span>
							<span class="token-chip token-whole">on</span>
							<span class="token-chip token-whole">the</span>
						</span>
						<span class="out-context-predict">???</span>
					</div>
					<div class="out-pipeline-steps">
						<div class="out-pipeline-step">
							<div class="out-pipe-label">Final residual vector</div>
							<div class="out-pipe-box out-pipe-residual">[&hellip;, 0.55, &minus;0.08, 0.41, &hellip;]<sub>d=768</sub></div>
						</div>
						<div class="out-pipeline-arrow">&darr; &times; W<sub>U</sub></div>
						<div class="out-pipeline-step">
							<div class="out-pipe-label">Raw logits</div>
							<div class="out-pipe-box out-pipe-logits" id="out-logits-display">[3.2, &minus;1.1, 0.4, 5.8, &hellip;]<sub>|V|=50k</sub></div>
						</div>
						<div class="out-pipeline-arrow">&darr; softmax(logits / T)</div>
						<div class="out-pipeline-step">
							<div class="out-pipe-label">Probabilities</div>
							<div class="out-pipe-box out-pipe-probs">P(next token | context)</div>
						</div>
					</div>

					<!-- Temperature control -->
					<div class="out-temp-control">
						<div class="out-temp-label" id="out-temp-display">Temperature: 1.0</div>
						<input type="range" min="0.1" max="3.0" step="0.1" value="1.0" class="out-temp-slider" id="out-temp-slider" />
						<div class="out-temp-range">
							<span>0.1 (deterministic)</span>
							<span>3.0 (creative)</span>
						</div>
					</div>

					<!-- Top-k predictions bar chart -->
					<div class="out-topk-container">
						<div class="out-topk-title">Top predicted tokens</div>
						<div class="out-topk-bars" id="out-topk-bars">
							<!-- Filled by JS -->
						</div>
						<div class="out-topk-rest" id="out-topk-rest"></div>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						At temperature 1.0 (the default), the model&rsquo;s raw confidence shows through &mdash;
						&ldquo;mat&rdquo; is the clear favorite for &ldquo;The cat sat on the ___&rdquo;. Lower the temperature
						toward 0.1 and the distribution collapses to near-certainty on &ldquo;mat.&rdquo; Raise it to 3.0
						and suddenly &ldquo;floor,&rdquo; &ldquo;bed,&rdquo; and other alternatives become plausible &mdash;
						the model becomes more <strong>creative</strong> but less <strong>predictable</strong>.
					</p>
					<p>
						This temperature parameter is what you&rsquo;re adjusting when you set &ldquo;temperature&rdquo; in
						an LLM API. At T=0, the model always picks the highest-probability token (greedy decoding).
						As T increases, sampling becomes more random, leading to more diverse and surprising text &mdash;
						but also more likely to produce nonsense.
					</p>
					<p>
						And that&rsquo;s the complete pipeline. A sequence of tokens enters the transformer, flows
						through embedding, positional encoding, N layers of attention and feed-forward processing
						(connected by the residual stream), and finally emerges as a probability distribution
						over the next token. The model generates text by sampling from this distribution,
						appending the chosen token, and repeating &mdash; autoregressively, one token at a time.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			{/* ===== Section 08b: Putting It All Together — Full Walkthrough ===== */}
